WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers episode 49,

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:08.020
its April 6th, 2026, starting the week with the latest from space and science.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.500
Artemis II astronauts have entered the moon's sphere of influence,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.760
marking humanity's return to lunar space.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.520
The four astronauts of NASA's Artemis II mission entered the lunar

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.900
sphere of influence early on April 6th.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.480
This sets up the first crude lunar flyby since 1972.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.700
The mission is successfully testing the Orion spacecraft in deep space.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.500
It serves as a critical rehearsal for the Artemis III lunar landing.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:36.040
Houston mission control is actively supporting the crew during this historic flight.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.620
The flight will test the Orion spacecraft in deep space ahead of Artemis III.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.620
This marks a major step in NASA's return to the moon.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.440
Houston has been cheering on the crew reclaiming its place as space city.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.720
I find it remarkable how these real-time connections between Earth and deep space

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.860
make the whole endeavor feel so immediate.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.840
While NASA pushes forward with crude exploration,

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.720
the agency is also facing some very earthly challenges back on the ground.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.220
NASA is on the brink of its greatest achievement in decades with Artemis.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.180
At the same time, the agency is confronting proposed science budget cuts.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:14.280
Administrator Jared Isaacman has defended a fiscal year 2027 budget proposal.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:18.920
That proposal would slash the agency's funding by nearly 25%.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.720
Work on the SLS Mobile Launcher 2 has been halted.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.940
The agency is shifting its launch architecture priorities.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.640
NASA has stopped work on a second mobile launch platform for an upgraded space launch system.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.400
It no longer plans to develop.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.040
The decision reflects shifting priorities in the agency's launch architecture.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.720
It raises questions about priorities in human space exploration.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.920
These budget realities show how even the most ambitious missions

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.080
must navigate political and financial constraints here on Earth.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.040
Even as budgets tighten international partners are finding new ways to work together in space.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.440
China and Europe have successfully launched an unusual collaborative space mission.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.960
The partnership marks a rare alignment between two of the world's major space powers.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.280
The collaboration represents an unusual partnership between major space powers.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:12.360
It highlights the evolving nature of international cooperation beyond traditional alliances.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.900
I am genuinely curious to see how this kind of cross-continental teamwork

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.540
might open new doors for future exploration.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.300
Space has always had a way of bringing unlikely players together when the goals are big enough.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.060
From large-scale human missions to the quiet beauty of Starbirth,

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.160
let us turn our attention to what the James Webb Space Telescope is revealing.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:38.800
The James Webb Space Telescope has spied once-hidden features in the W-51 star-forming region.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.080
It used infrared light to pierce dense clouds of gas and dust.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:48.140
Combined with Alma millimeter observations, the telescopes are revealing proto-stars.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.820
They are also showing outflows and complex chemistry previously hidden from view.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.140
What looked like an opaque nursery is now revealing dramatic processes.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.480
These processes remain invisible to optical telescopes.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.620
The new images show the messy energetic environment where new solar systems are taking shape.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:09.260
Alma complements this by observing at millimeter wavelengths.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.660
Together, they paint a fuller picture of how stars emerge from their natal clouds.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:19.280
This view helps us understand the energetic chaos that eventually leads to stable planetary systems

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:25.400
like our own. Speaking of star formation, one of our listeners recently asked a fascinating question

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:31.100
about how proto-stars actually evolve. Now, here is something that blew my mind when I looked into it.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:37.340
Early proto-stars are fully convective and contract almost isothermally along the Hayashi track.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.680
If you could shrink the sun down to the size of a basketball, a typical proto-star on the

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:48.280
Hayashi track would still be roughly the volume of a small house. Yet, it would contain almost the same mass.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:54.580
The young star is fully convective like a boiling pot, where giant churning cells carry heat from

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.880
the center all the way to the surface. Because this convection is so efficient, the star can contract

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.620
while radiating energy outward. It still keeps its surface temperature almost constant.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.140
That is why it slides down the Hayashi track on the Hurtsprung Russell diagram.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.540
The heat is carried by massive blobs of plasma rising and falling, so the interior stays well mixed

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:21.480
and the surface can stay cool even as gravity squeezes the star smaller. Then something changes once

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:27.100
the core grows dense and hot enough, opacity drops and radiation takes over as the main way energy

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:32.120
moves outward. A radiative core forms because photons can now travel through the hotter,

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:37.140
more transparent gas, they do so without being constantly absorbed and re-emitted. On the

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.960
Hanyi track, the star contracts at nearly constant luminosity. Its surface heats up because the energy

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.280
is now flowing through a stable radiative interior instead of being dumped at the surface by convection.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.160
Temperatures inside these cores can reach millions of degrees. This happens on time scales of hundreds

00:04:54.160 --> 00:05:00.220
of thousands of years. Yet, we still do not fully understand exactly what tips the balance between

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.940
convective and radiative transport. This is especially true in stars of different masses between

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:11.280
half a solar mass and three solar masses. That precise switch continues to challenge our models.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:15.780
The transition determines how quickly the star will eventually ignite hydrogen fusion.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.920
Then it settles onto the main sequence. Think about that for a second. The entire fate of a newborn

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:26.520
star hinges on this subtle change in how it carries heat. It is one of those beautiful details that

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:31.660
makes stellar evolution feel both predictable and mysterious at the same time. While we study

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:36.820
distant star forming regions, some very human and surprisingly practical challenges are making headlines

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:42.520
on the Artemis 2 mission. The Artemis 2 crew has been dealing with Microsoft Outlook issues while in

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:48.440
space. This highlights the unexpected software challenges of long duration, human spaceflight.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:52.960
Toilet troubles have reportedly been the biggest headache of the current lunar mission.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:58.300
This detail underscores the practical engineering realities of crew deep space travel.

